    <!-- ── Feature deep-dive: three text blocks ── -->
    <div class="ops-features-grid">

      <article class="ops-feature-block">
        <h3>Memberships that handle their own lifecycle</h3>
        <p>
          Running a club means dealing with memberships that change state constantly —
          members upgrade, pause during a holiday, miss a payment, add a family member.
          Pagayo handles every transition in the background: TRIALING grants a grace
          period, PAST_DUE starts a dunning sequence, PAUSED suspends scan access
          without losing the member's history.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every member gets a unique pass code linked to their subscription. The scan
          desk validates it in real time — and blocks entry the moment the membership
          goes EXPIRED, CANCELED or PAST_DUE. No spreadsheet. No “I'll let you in this
          one time”. No revenue leak.
        </p>
        <ul class="ops-bullet-list">
          <li>Automatic dunning — email reminders at 3, 7, and 14 days overdue</li>
          <li>Family memberships — multiple members per subscription holder</li>
          <li>Pause &amp; resume without losing payment history</li>
          <li>Upgrade and downgrade mid-cycle with pro-rata calculation</li>
          <li>Scan access log per member per day — full visit history</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="ops-feature-block">
        <h3>One order queue. Front desk, webshop, everything.</h3>
        <p>
          Most gym software treats the front desk and the webshop as separate worlds,
          then sells you an integration to bolt them back together. Pagayo uses one
          order model for every channel: the receptionist selling a protein shake,
          a member buying a class pack online, a coach taking a walk-in payment via
          QR — all land in the same queue, with the same status flow.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every transaction is tagged with its <code>source</code> (POS, WEB, CASH, WHATSAPP)
          and <code>originator</code> (CUSTOMER, SELLER, SYSTEM), so the audit trail is
          always honest. Refunds follow a four-step process — requested, approved,
          received, refunded — with credit notes generated automatically.
        </p>
        <ul class="ops-bullet-list">
          <li>Unified order queue — no separate POS and webshop reconciliation</li>
          <li>QR-code checkout for day passes and class packs</li>
          <li>Partial refunds and store credit</li>
          <li>VAT grouping per product category</li>
          <li>Daily end-of-day reconciliation report</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="ops-feature-block">
        <h3>Stock, coupons and the numbers that matter</h3>
        <p>
          Clubs don't just sell memberships. They sell merchandise, supplements, class
          packs and day passes. Pagayo tracks inventory per variant, fires low-stock
          alerts when thresholds are crossed, and estimates days-to-stockout from your
          actual sales velocity — not a static reorder rule from 1998.
        </p>
        <p>
          Coupon campaigns can be time-limited, segment-specific, or tied to a
          referral source. Usage analytics show redemption rate, average uplift and
          net revenue impact per code — so you can tell which promotions actually
          drove revenue and which just gave away margin.
        </p>
        <ul class="ops-bullet-list">
          <li>Inventory low-stock alerts with configurable thresholds</li>
          <li>Coupon codes — percentage or fixed, single-use or multi-use</li>
          <li>Revenue breakdown by membership vs. merchandise vs. classes</li>
          <li>Monthly recurring revenue (MRR) tracker</li>
          <li>Churn rate and net revenue retention per plan tier</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
